    "staticBio:wrap": {
      "x": 0.04775,
      "y": 0.507,
      "align": "left",
      "hidden": false,
      "letterSpacing": 0,
      "text": "In 1977, 18-year-old Canadian Terry Fox was diagnosed with bone cancer, and his right leg was amputated in order to treat the cancer. Terry ran his Marathon of Hope in 1980 - a cross-Canada run on one good leg and one artificial leg to raise money for cancer research. After running an average of a marathon a day for 143 days in a row, totalling 3,339 miles, Terry ended his run because his cancer had returned. But his courage, determination, and compassion began a worldwide movement to eradicate cancer. Terry Fox passed away in 1981, and now millions of people in over 40 countries participate in his name to raise funds for cancer research.",
